WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.226
Traditional antivirus focused heavily on identifying known malicious files, often using signatures.

2
00:00:06.226 --> 00:00:07.543
That is still useful.

3
00:00:07.543 --> 00:00:13.071
Known malware should be blocked when possible, and prevention remains an important security layer.

4
00:00:13.071 --> 00:00:16.563
Attackers do not always rely on obvious malware files.

5
00:00:16.563 --> 00:00:23.298
They may use legitimate tools, scripts, stolen credentials, or built-in operating system features.

6
00:00:23.298 --> 00:00:25.793
EDR looks more broadly at behavior.

7
00:00:25.793 --> 00:00:35.990
A strange PowerShell command, a process dumping credentials, an unusual parent-child process relationship, or a suspicious outbound connection may all matter.

8
00:00:35.990 --> 00:00:38.195
EDR does not replace prevention.

9
00:00:38.195 --> 00:00:44.651
It complements prevention by helping defenders detect and investigate activity that gets past earlier controls.

10
00:00:44.651 --> 00:00:55.367
The practical difference is that older antivirus often asks whether a file is known to be bad, while EDR also asks what the endpoint is doing and whether that behavior makes sense.
